WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
A DICTATORSHIP:
Founders have dictatorized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:10.020
companies. Back on September 16, 1985,&nbsp;
Steve Jobs was fired from his own company:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:17.280
Apple. Fans like to argue that this was completely&nbsp;
unjust and unfair, and from a moral perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.840
they’re right. It is unfair to kick out a founder&nbsp;
from their own company, but it was also effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.900
While Apple got destroyed over the next decade,&nbsp;
Steve Jobs had the opportunity to grow as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.900
leader, a family man, and a visionary. And when he&nbsp;
returned 12 years later, he was able to make Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.080
into the world’s largest company. Looking back,&nbsp;
even Jobs himself has agreed that getting fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.540
was the medicine that he needed. This isn’t to&nbsp;
say that all founders should be fired from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.600
companies to be grounded and put in their place.&nbsp;
But, what it does show is that even the smartest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.240
minds in the world are just straight up wrong&nbsp;
from time to time. And this is precisely why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.780
large public companies have boards, chairmans,&nbsp;
shareholders, and an entire executive team. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.820
idea is that there’s a system of checks and&nbsp;
balances that ensure that no single person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:19.440
becomes too powerful, but this is no longer&nbsp;
the case. Modern companies still have boards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.860
and investors and executive teams but all of&nbsp;
that is just for show. At the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.820
the person who has all the power is the founder or&nbsp;
founders. Now, this is no problem if we’re talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:39.780
about a private company or the given founders have&nbsp;
a stake of over 50%. But with modern tech giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.720
this is not the case. Take Mark Zuckerberg&nbsp;
for example. Over the past few years, he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.480
been gambling tens of billion on the Metaverse,&nbsp;
something that investors haven’t exactly been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.880
excited about. To make things worse, much of their&nbsp;
launched Metaverse products have flopped beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:04.500
belief boasting as little as 38 users. All of this&nbsp;
has given Zuckerberg a favorability rating of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:11.700
22% or only half of even Donald Trump. This&nbsp;
is precisely the type of situation in which a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:19.740
CEO would be fired, but Mark doesn’t have to worry&nbsp;
about that. You see, Mark only owns 13.6% of Meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.980
not much more than Steve Jobs’ stake of 11%&nbsp;
when he was fired. So, nowhere near a majority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:32.340
but see, modern founders have learned from their&nbsp;
predecessors. They know what can happen if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.020
give away majority control. So, while they may&nbsp;
give away a majority stake in their company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.800
they never give away majority control. How you&nbsp;
ask? Well, they’ve brilliantly made a distinction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:52.020
between equity and voting power. While Zuckerberg&nbsp;
only owns 13% of Meta, he controls 54% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:58.920
voting power. A similar story can be seen with&nbsp;
Google. At this point, Larry Page only owns 6.1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:07.620
and Sergey Brin only owns 5.7%. But, together,&nbsp;
they control 51.2% of Google’s voting power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:15.540
This means that despite have single digit stakes,&nbsp;
this duo has ultimate control of a $1.3 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:22.920
company with 180,000 employees, tens of millions&nbsp;
of investors, and billions of users as this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.400
story of how founders abused their power. This is&nbsp;
the story of how founders dictatorized companies.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.100
A BANNED PRACTICE:&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:41.700
The root of modern day founders’ power is thanks&nbsp;
to multiple classes of stock with different voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.520
powers. Class A stock might come along with 1 vote&nbsp;
per share while Class B stock comes along with 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.980
votes per share. The average investor doesn’t&nbsp;
even realize this, but many companies actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.320
have different classes of stock on the public&nbsp;
market itself. Likely the best example of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.260
none other than Google. If you look up Google on&nbsp;
your brokerage, you may come across the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:12.420
listing: Alphabet Inc Class C, ticker symbol GOOG.&nbsp;
At first glance, it looks like Google stock. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:18.900
market cap checks out, the PE ratio checks out,&nbsp;
the price looks about right. And that’s because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:25.500
is technically, Google stock, but what it isn’t is&nbsp;
Google stock with voting shares. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.440
you can buy a billion of these shares and invest&nbsp;
a $100 billion, but you wouldn’t even get a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:38.700
vote. If you want voting power, you need to buy&nbsp;
Alphabet Inc Class A stock or ticker symbol GOOGL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:44.460
Basically everything about this share is identical&nbsp;
to the Class C shares, except you’ll get 1 vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:51.420
for every share that you own. But, the shares that&nbsp;
you really want are Class B shares. With Class B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.900
shares, you get 10 votes for every share you own.&nbsp;
But, you can look all you want on any brokerage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:04.680
you can find, but you’ll never find Class B&nbsp;
Google shares, as Class B shares are reserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:11.580
for founders, VC firms, and other close insiders.&nbsp;
Most investors don’t look into this too much. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:17.820
figure that their 1000 or even 100,000 votes won't&nbsp;
really make a difference at Google. And they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.700
right, as an individual, they can’t really make a&nbsp;
difference at these companies. But the fact that 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:30.300
or 2 people have all the votes that everyone gives&nbsp;
up does make a difference, and that’s why multi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:39.240
class stock structures were banned for over 50&nbsp;
years. You see, multi class stock is by no means a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:45.240
new invention. It’s actually been around for well&nbsp;
over a hundred years, but it was a frowned upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.460
invention. The first American company to issue&nbsp;
multi class stock was the International Silver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:57.000
Company back in 1898. Originally, this wasn’t&nbsp;
considered to be a big deal. Founders wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.060
a bit more control over their companies and given&nbsp;
that information wasn’t nearly as accessible as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:10.020
is today, it made sense for investors to be more&nbsp;
passive. But, over time, founders started becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:16.800
more and more aggressive. What started off as 1&nbsp;
extra vote became 5 extra votes and then 10 extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:23.580
votes and then 20 extra votes. This practice only&nbsp;
became worse with the roaring 1920s as everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:29.520
people started buying stocks. They didn’t care&nbsp;
about voting shares or earnings reports, they just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.700
wanted to be a part of the Rockefeller fortune&nbsp;
or the Ford fortune. This gave companies plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:42.540
of room to test the boundaries until one company&nbsp;
pushed it way too far: Dodge Motor Company. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:49.560
1925, Dodge would have one of the most infamous&nbsp;
IPOs of all time. They would exclusively issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.800
shares with absolutely no voting power. This meant&nbsp;
that the insiders could own just a single share of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:03.960
stock and retain 100% voting power, and that’s&nbsp;
exactly what they did too. The insiders owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:12.540
just 1.7% of the company had absolute control over&nbsp;
the company. Investors were obviously pissed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:18.360
this led to an uproar on Wall Street. The New York&nbsp;
Stock Exchange would end up siding with investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:25.920
and in 1926, they would officially ban dual class&nbsp;
stock. This is where the story should’ve ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.680
but this practice has not only been revived,&nbsp;
but it has taken over the market once again.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:40.140
REVIVING THE DEAD:
Throughout the next 54 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:45.360
the stock market operated perfectly fine.&nbsp;
Billionaire founders were made, millionaire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.460
investors were made, and everyone had completely&nbsp;
forgotten that dual class stock was even a thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:58.380
but this would all change on February 8, 1971.&nbsp;
This was the day that the NASDAQ was created. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.760
you’re not familiar with the NASDAQ, you should&nbsp;
check out our video about them but long story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:08.760
short, they technolofied the stock market. Before&nbsp;
the NASDAQ, investors had to call stock brokers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:14.220
at the NYSE and place buy and sell orders&nbsp;
and everything would be done manually. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.160
with the NASDAQ, the entire stock market was made&nbsp;
electronic paving the road for how it is today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:27.780
This was obviously the superior technology but&nbsp;
investors weren’t impressed. They couldn’t care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:33.360
less if quotes and trades was electronic or not.&nbsp;
In fact, they preferred to call a broker that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:39.600
knew and trusted for 20 years. So, despite&nbsp;
offering great tech, the NASDAQ struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.940
to get investors throughout the 1970s. To make&nbsp;
things worse, the NYSE had started ripping off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:51.060
NASDAQ’s tech and were rapidly modernizing their&nbsp;
own systems. So, things looked pretty bleak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:57.060
the NASDAQ. Investors weren’t using the exchange&nbsp;
and their main advantage was rapidly disappearing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:02.700
So, in the early 1980s, they would decide to&nbsp;
pivot their strategy. Instead of trying to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:08.460
investors, they started going after companies. If&nbsp;
companies were exclusively listed on the NASDAQ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.700
investors had no choice but to buy through the&nbsp;
NASDAQ. With that, the NASDAQ started charging far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:21.480
lower fees than the NYSE for companies and they&nbsp;
still do to this day, but this too didn’t work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:28.320
You see, there was a certain prestige and heritage&nbsp;
to the NYSE. JP Morgan Chase wasn’t gonna switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:35.280
exchanges just because you charged less. And with&nbsp;
that, the NASDAQ was back to ground 0. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.840
worse off actually as they were now earning&nbsp;
lower fees as well. Throughout all of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.760
the NASDAQ couldn’t help but notice that one&nbsp;
exchange was actually succeeding at taking on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:51.120
the NYSE: the AMEX, not American Express, but&nbsp;
the American Stock Exchange. One of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:57.060
factors that distinguished the AMEX from the NYSE&nbsp;
was their policy on dual class stock. The AMEX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:03.660
allowed for DCS with a voting ratio of up to 10&nbsp;
to 1. The NASDAQ didn’t have any DCS restrictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:08.220
to begin with, but now they knew that this&nbsp;
was precisely what they needed to lean into.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.980
There were two target markets with this strategy.&nbsp;
The first was tech companies who were fearful that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:19.500
investors weren’t really informed on tech which&nbsp;
was very much true at the time. The second was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.520
companies that were targets of corporate&nbsp;
raidings. If these companies were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:28.140
distinguish between ownership and voting power,&nbsp;
they could protect themselves from corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.060
raiders. Given that corporate raiding was at its&nbsp;
peak throughout the 80s and that tech stocks would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:39.360
go vertical in the 90s, the NASDAQ would also&nbsp;
go vertical. Seeing this, companies listed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.000
the NYSE would threaten to switch to the NASDAQ&nbsp;
if they didn’t allow for DCS. And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:52.920
the NYSE had no choice but to unban DCS in 1986.&nbsp;
With that being said though, companies were pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:58.140
reasonable in the early days. In fact, some&nbsp;
notable NASDAQ companies didn’t even opt for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:04.260
dual class shares like Apple and Microsoft. But,&nbsp;
there’s always someone who ruins it for everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.640
else. And in the case of dual class stock, it&nbsp;
was our good old friends Google and Facebook.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.180
ULTIMATE CONTROL:&nbsp;

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:22.200
Nowadays, most tech companies opt for a&nbsp;
dual class structure because I mean why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.240
wouldn’t they. The average investor isn’t even&nbsp;
aware of it, the practice has been normalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.920
and it gives founders ultimate control.&nbsp;
It’s one of those things where it’s like:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:38.160
if no one is opposing it, why would you not&nbsp;
do it? Some notable tech companies who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:44.640
adopted this practice are Snapchat, Dropbox,&nbsp;
Lyft, Kayak, Robinhood, Coinbase, Zoom, Roku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.100
and Trip Advisor, but this practive is by no&nbsp;
means limited to tech companies anymore. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:57.420
legacy companies leveraging DCS include Coca Cola,&nbsp;
Ford, Nike, Levi, and Hyatt Hotels. Also, don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:02.040
bet on media companies to report on this issue&nbsp;
as the most notable ones leverage it themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:09.000
including the New York Times, Fox News, CBS, and&nbsp;
Comcast. And there are of course extreme examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:15.120
as well. The Ford Family for example owns 4% of&nbsp;
the stock but control 40% of the voting power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:22.980
Similarly, Echostar CEO Charlie Ergen owns 5%&nbsp;
of the company but controls 90% of the vote. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:28.680
let’s just say things have gotten pretty bad, but&nbsp;
I do want to point out some notable exceptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.720
starting with Amazon. While Jeff Bezos&nbsp;
gets a bunch of critique for his practices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:44.880
this is not one of them. Another exception is Elon&nbsp;
Musk and Tesla. In fact, this is why Elon was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:50.400
to be forced to give up his chairman position in&nbsp;
2018. And as for a startup that’s going against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:57.660
the trend, we have Uber. So, the good news is that&nbsp;
some companies are doing the right thing. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:05.760
the S&amp;P 500 is putting up some resistance as well.&nbsp;
Starting in 2017, they ruled that companies with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:11.820
multi class stock will no longer be added to&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500. This is the main reason that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:17.880
notable startups like Snapchat have been unable&nbsp;
to join the index despite their size. And given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:23.340
that index fund investing is on the rise, newer&nbsp;
companies will very much be losing out if they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:30.120
not part of the index. Will this be enough to stop&nbsp;
the practive altogether? Probably not, but it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:34.860
step in the right direction. Did you realize how&nbsp;
these founders were monopolizing voting power?&nbsp;&nbsp;

